# --- Large data files (keep out of git; ship separately as the dataset zip) ---
data/raw/2015.csv          # 517 MB extracted raw BRFSS survey
*.csv
data/raw/*.zip             # 79 MB raw source archive
archive/                   # old readmission project + its dataset (not part of submission)

# ...but the CLEANED table (17 MB) is the input notebook 02 needs, and the README promises it
# is in the repo so a grader can skip notebook 01 (which needs the 528 MB raw file). The blanket
# *.csv rule above was silently excluding it, so re-include it explicitly.
!data/processed/diabetes_2015_clean.csv

# --- Jupyter / Python cruft ---
.ipynb_checkpoints/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.virtual_documents/

# --- Clinic web app runtime state (regenerated by the app / seed_demo) ---
data/clinic_db.json

# --- Deployment artifact: committed on purpose, but check the size ---
# models/*.joblib is deliberately NOT ignored. §6.4b rule 3 selects among equally accurate
# models by which is cheapest to ship, so the exported artifact is small enough to live in
# the repo — which is what makes a fresh clone runnable without a 75-minute notebook run.
# This was not always true: an earlier run selected a Random Forest whose artifact was
# ~340 MB, past GitHub's 100 MB per-file limit, which would have made `git push` fail. That
# episode is what rule 3 exists to prevent, and §12a now prints the measured size plus a
# version-control verdict on every run. If it ever warns that the artifact is too large,
# add `models/*.joblib` here and ship the file in the submission zip instead.

# --- Secrets: local environment file (never commit real keys) ---
.env

# --- OS ---
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
